entry cosmic-radiation
term: Cosmic Radiation
image: ../../assets/images/terms/radiation.webp
related_terms: background-radiation
related_terms: ionizing-radiation
related_terms: radiation
related_terms: exposure
quick_facts: Origins: supernovae across the galaxy, plus solar storms
quick_facts: At ground level we're hit mainly by secondary shower particles
quick_facts: Dose roughly doubles every 2 km of altitude
quick_facts: Airline crews get the highest occupational doses in civilian work
quick_facts: Earth's atmosphere and magnetic field are our shields

sources:
    {"organization":"NASA","title":"Space Radiation Analysis Group","url":"https://srag.jsc.nasa.gov/"}

definition:
    High-energy particles — mostly protons and atomic nuclei — arriving from outside the solar system (galactic cosmic rays, born in supernovae) plus bursts of solar particles from the Sun. When they smash into the atmosphere they trigger showers of secondary particles; those secondaries, not the originals, account for nearly all cosmic dose at ground level. Exposure roughly doubles every 2,000 meters of altitude, which is why airline crews receive among the highest occupational doses of any profession.

debunking_snippet:
    “Cosmic radiation is just sunlight.” It isn't light at all — it's charged particles, mostly from far beyond the solar system, moving at nearly light speed.
